#==============================================================================
# Nuxel LUKS2 userdata crypttab template — mustang
#
# Installed to /etc/crypttab on the device rootfs (via initramfs rewrite at
# firstboot). It defines how the LUKS2-encrypted userdata partition is opened
# before the userdata filesystem (f2fs/userdata) is mounted.
#
# Template markers (@@...@@) are expanded at build time or by
# images/hooks/luks-userdata.sh at runtime:
#   @@USERDATA_DEV@@  → the userdata block device (typically /dev/sda on
#                       mustang's UFS, or /dev/disk/by-partlabel/userdata)
#   @@KEYFILE@@       → path to the key file (optional)
#
# Two auth strategies are supported (see images/hooks/luks-userdata.sh):
#   1. Passphrase prompt  → the initramfs drops to a prompt (keyfile unset).
#   2. Key file           → a key file derived from the AVB-verified boot chain
#                           + disk serial (recommended for unlocked-boot /
#                           first-unlock automount). Set @@KEYFILE@@ to
#                           /etc/nuxel/userdata.key.
#
# IMPORTANT: This file is the SHIPPED TEMPLATE (`.in`). Do NOT install the
# literal "@@...@@" markers into /etc/crypttab on a real device — expand them
# first (images/hooks/luks-userdata.sh performs expansion at initramfs time).
#==============================================================================

# <name>                <device>            <password>                       <cipher-options>
userdata                @@USERDATA_DEV@@    @@KEYFILE@@                      luks,discard

# --- Reference options ---
# Passphrase-prompt variant (KEYFILE empty):
#   userdata  @@USERDATA_DEV@@  none  luks,discard
#   (with 'none' the initramfs prompts for a passphrase interactively)
#
# Keyfile variant pointing at a hardened-malloc protected key:
#   userdata  @@USERDATA_DEV@@  /etc/nuxel/userdata.key  luks,discard
#
# To also verify integrity on top of confidentiality (LUKS2 with dm-integrity):
#   userdata  @@USERDATA_DEV@@  none  luks,discard,integrity
#
# On the VM (plain LVM userdata, no encryption), this file is NOT used; the VM
# mounts its plain LV directly. See docs/update-strategy.md §2.5.
